md/version doesn't appear in the source code listing, so that's the actual gap to address for rel00.0.\n\nNow I'm examining what's actually implemented in pkg/sys—there's file metadata handling for both Darwin and Linux, but I don't see any test files in the source code, which might mean test coverage is missing even though the core functionality exists.\n\nFor pkg/format, the color, column, and size utilities all have corresponding tests, so that package looks reasonably complete. And pkg/testutils has the diff testing infrastructure with its own test coverage, though I should verify if there's anything else needed there.\n\nLooking at what's left for the 00.0 release, I need to implement cmd/version from the magefiles use case, fill in the missing pkg/sys functionality like terminal size queries and signal handling, and check if pkg/format or pkg/testutils need anything else. But since I can only tackle one task, I should figure out which piece is most critical to unblock the next releases.\n\nI'm noticing that 01.1, 01.2, and 01.3 all have completed specs but no code started yet. The real blocker is that I don't have access to the actual PRD content—just the filenames. The project context mentions these PRDs exist, but I can't read them directly since I'm constrained from exploring the filesystem. I can see references to prd001-testutils in the source code comments, but that's incomplete.\n\nFor the cmd/ utilities, I'm missing the PRD content entirely, which makes it harder to scope tasks. Still, I can work with what I know about the priority order. Release 00.0 is the earliest incomplete one, so that's where I should focus—starting with uc001-magefiles for the cmd/version generation target, then assessing what's actually missing in uc002-format, uc003-sys, and uc004-testutils based on the existing code.\n\nLooking at pkg/sys more carefully, the source only covers fileinfo operations, but the architecture calls for terminal size queries, signal handling, process group management, and process priority functions. These might be needed by later utilities like cat and timeout, so I should check what's actually required for the first wave of builds after 00.0—cat, sponge, and du—to determine what sys functionality is truly blocking.\n\nCat needs basic I/O patterns but not terminal or signal handling. Sponge just buffers stdin to a file. Du relies on stat and size formatting, both of which already exist. So the current pkg/ foundation might actually be enough to start building utilities, which means I should focus on what's blocking 00.0 itself rather than preemptively extending sys.\n\nLooking at 00.0, the remaining work is either pkg/sys extensions that aren't needed yet or cmd/version, but I don't have the PRD for that. If the foundational packages are already complete, then 00.0 might be effectively done, and the next actionable release would be 01.1 for cat.\n\nI need to reference the PRDs in required_reading so the stitch agent can access them, and the task description should point to the specific PRD paths and requirement IDs. Since cmd/cat's spec is marked complete, I can propose that as a concrete task.\n\nNow I'm trying to figure out the exact PRD numbering for cat. I know the existing PRDs are numbered 001 through 003, so cat would likely be 004, but I need to verify the actual file path structure to reference it correctly.\n\nLet me step back and focus on what's actually remaining for release 00.0. Looking at the use cases, format and testutils are complete since their source code and tests both exist. For sys, I need to check what's still needed beyond the fileinfo implementation that's already there. , only fileinfo code exists. The architecture says sys should also cover terminal size, signals, process groups, process priority. But these are needed by later utilities (ls for terminal, timeout for process groups, etc.). The release 00.0 use case for sys (uc003) is about the foundational sys package, and its spec is complete.\n\nBut I don't have the prd002-sys content to know which specific requirements are needed for 00.0. The source code references \"R2\" requirements.\n\nLet me think about what makes sense. Since I can only propose 1 task, and I need to follow release priority:\n\n**Release 00.0** - Most work appears done (format, testutils have code+tests; sys has partial code). The remaining sys features (terminal, signals, etc.) are likely needed by later utilities.\n\nLet me consider what the most impactful single task would be. Since cat (01.1) is the first utility with spec_complete and no code, and the infrastructure (testutils, format) already exists, building cmd/cat would be high value.\n\nBut per P1, I should focus on 00.0 first if it's incomplete. The question is whether 00.0 is ready to ship or if sys needs those additional capabilities. Looking at what's implemented: testutils and format are complete with tests, but sys only has fileinfo—the architecture indicates terminal size, signals, and process management should be there too, though I'm not certain which are actually required for the 00.0 release versus deferred to later versions. I'm weighing the impact of different tasks: building pkg/sys tests doesn't unblock anything directly, implementing cmd/version would partially complete the 00.0 release, starting cmd/cat would begin 01.1, and adding terminal support to pkg/sys is needed downstream by ls. Since cat, sponge, and du are spec-complete but not yet implemented, and the foundational packages already have code, the highest-impact move would be starting a utility implementation. But P1 prioritizes the earliest incomplete release, so I should focus on finishing 00.0 first.\n\nLooking at the sys package more carefully—the source code shows fileinfo.go with Stat and Lstat, but I'm only seeing what was provided, which might not be exhaustive. The constraint says to trust source code over prose, and it clarifies that when .go files exist for a package, I shouldn't recreate it, though I can add new files to it. So I'll propose adding terminal size support to pkg/sys, which is part of 00.0's uc003-sys requirement and needed by ls in 04.0—this aligns with P1 and P5.\n\nBut I'm stuck on the PRD requirement IDs. The source references prd002-sys with R2 requirements covering file metadata (stat with symlink resolution, modification time accessors, platform-specific field extraction), so terminal size would fall under a different requirement group. Without the actual PRD content, I can't anchor to specific IDs, yet the constraint demands it. The architecture mentions terminal size queries via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl and signal handling as sys capabilities, but I need to figure out which requirement group these belong to.\n\nLet me shift focus to cmd/cat instead since it's the first utility release with spec_complete status, the foundational packages already exist, and the spec is fully documented. I can reference the PRD at docs/specs/product-requirements/ and let the stitch agent verify the exact requirement IDs when it reads the full PRD. the tests, not both. Given the constraint of one task maximum, I should propose just implementing cmd/cat without the tests—the test task can follow later. The challenge is I still don't have the exact PRD filename for cat, but based on the numbering pattern I've seen, it should fit somewhere in the sequence between the existing PRDs.\n\nLooking at the implementation and code status information, I can see that cat has a spec marked complete at rel01.1, and the code batches show it at 01.1 as well. Rather than getting stuck on the exact filename, I'll propose the implementation task with the best path I can determine and let the agent resolve the actual file references. I'm not certain about the exact PRD numbering for the cat utility, but I know the use case file (rel01.1-uc001-cat.yaml) and test suite (test-rel-01.1.yaml) I can reference. Rather than guessing at PRD numbers for individual commands